\section{Value of information and misspecification}

\begin{theorem}[Concavity]
For weight vectors $q_1,q_2$ and $t\in[0,1]$: $t\opt(q_1)+(1-t)\opt(q_2)\le\opt(tq_1+(1-t)q_2)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$C(\cdot,\sigma)$ is linear for fixed $\sigma$; minimize over $\sigma$.
\end{proof}

With Theorems~\ref{thm:greedy} and \ref{thm:opt}: if $\alpha\ge w$ then $\opt(h)=\hint(M,W,\alpha)$.

\begin{theorem}[Misspecified-coverage regret]
If coverage $\alpha$ is claimed but the truth is $\beta$, the committed cost exceeds the advertised one by exactly $(\alpha-\beta)M/2$, and the realized speedup is $(M+1)/((1-\beta)M+W+1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:exact} at $\beta$ and $\alpha$ and subtract.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict suboptimality under over-trust]
If the true coverage satisfies $\beta M<W$, then $\opt(h^{(\beta)})<C(h^{(\beta)},\id)$ whatever was claimed.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} at $\beta$.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Bayes-optimal hinted search.} Form the truthful posterior $h_j=\alpha\pi_j/\pi(S)$ on the window $S$ and $(1-\alpha)\pi_j/\pi(S^c)$ off it, sort by non-increasing $h_j$, probe in that order ($O(M\log M)$; optimal by Theorem~\ref{thm:greedy}). Under the uniform prior with $\alpha\ge w$ the window-first order is already greedy ($O(M)$).

\textbf{Hint pricing and calibration.} $S=(M+1)/((1-\alpha)M+W+1)$; an observed $S^\ast$ at width $w$ corresponds to coverage $\alpha^\ast=1-1/S^\ast+w$, and at coverage $\alpha$ to width $w^\ast=1/S^\ast-(1-\alpha)$.

\textbf{Exhaustive verification.} For small $M$, enumerate all $M!$ orders in exact arithmetic ($O(M!\,M)$).

\section{Numerical illustrations}

\emph{Convergence.} At $w=0.05$, $\alpha=0.9$ the exact speedups are $5.500$ ($M=10$), $6.3125$ ($M=100$), $6.6291$ ($M=1000$), $6.66629$ ($M=10^5$), approaching $1/0.15=6.6667$ from below at rate $O(1/M)$.

\emph{Ceilings.} At $M=1000$, $\alpha=0.9$, shrinking $W$ from $20$ to $1$ raises $S$ only from $8.27$ to $9.81$ (coverage ceiling $10$); at $W=200$, $\alpha=0.99$ gives $4.74$ (width ceiling $4.98$).

\emph{Misspecification.} At $M=1000$, $W=30$, claimed $\alpha=0.9$ advertises $65.5$ probes; true $\beta=0.7$ gives $165.5$ (exactly $100$ more); true $\beta=0.02<w$ gives $505.5$ against a Bayes optimum of $495.5$ and a blind cost of $500.5$.

\emph{Monte Carlo.} $2\times10^5$ simulated searches at $M=200$, $W=10$, $\alpha=0.9$ give mean $15.40$ probes against exact $15.5$; gaps such as $29.1$ versus $34.0$ in the min-law table are not sampling error.

\section{Relation to classical search theory}

Theorem~\ref{thm:greedy} is the classical rule for optimal search of a stationary target with perfect detection. The novelty lies in the parametric family of side information and its closed forms. Concavity of the Bayes cost is the search-theoretic face of the concavity of Bayes risk in the prior that underlies the comparison of experiments. The min-law models the smaller of two unknown quantities, such as the smaller of two hidden factors.

\section{Discussion}

Positional information is a two-parameter object whose parameters combine through the residual $1-\alpha+w$. Comparing hints reduces to comparing $(1-\alpha)M+W$; an observed speedup corresponds to a line $\alpha-w=1-1/S^\ast$ of equivalent hints rather than a point; and trusting the window first is optimal only above $\alpha=w$. In the motivating study, two earlier models were rejected because exact computation and simulation disagreed: a simulation that ignored coverage, and a ``uniform given a hit'' model contradicted by the monotonicity of the min-law.

\emph{Limitations.} Exact imperfect-coverage results are for the uniform prior; min-law results are for perfect coverage with aligned blocks.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Additive law for monotone priors:} for non-increasing priors and $\alpha\ge w$, conjecturally $S=1/((1-\alpha)c_0+wc_1)$ with shape constants $c_0,c_1$.
\item \emph{Sharp threshold for general priors:} window-first is optimal iff $\alpha\min_S\pi/\pi(S)\ge(1-\alpha)\max_{S^c}\pi/\pi(S^c)$.
\item \emph{Min-law with imperfect coverage:} an explicit rational $S(\alpha,w)$ reducing to $2/(w(3-w))$ at $\alpha=1$.
\item \emph{Information ordering:} hint $A$ dominates $B$ for every prior iff $A$'s posterior partition refines $B$'s in Blackwell's sense; concavity gives one direction.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

An interval hint is worth exactly two numbers; under a uniform prior its speedup is $(M+1)/((1-\alpha)M+W+1)\to1/(1-\alpha+w)$. Coverage and width trade at a fixed exchange rate, each imposes its own ceiling, and window-first is optimal exactly when coverage exceeds width. These facts recalibrate an observed $5.19\times$ gain to $82$--$86\%$ coverage and give the exact min-law perfect-coverage speedup $2/(w(3-w))$.

